#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470 :topic/difficulty 1.147700024049995 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429503 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 57->1114. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} :encompassing/weight 0.3333333333333333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429504 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 57->280. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} :encompassing/weight 1.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429505 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 2 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1, 2]; waits [1, 1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (2/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 57->332. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "f7decca1-c481-59f9-84e3-bd90fac3ecc8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054572 :question/id #uuid "af328f52-53a1-5cc1-a542-6cad8eea4c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-6114" :question/problem "Given $xy+y^2=x^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054503 :question/id #uuid "59d2e907-e788-5a22-93a9-c49ff94df6ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-190" :question/problem "Given ${x}^{2} + {y}^{2} = \\sin   ( y ) ,$ find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ 2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} \\\\ 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} - \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ ( 2 y - \\cos   ( y ) ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - \\frac{2 x}{2 y - \\cos   ( y )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\frac{2 x}{\\cos   ( y ) - 2 y}\\end{array}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054504 :question/id #uuid "8a6eb51f-6232-5a08-acf1-136f0d14a4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-191" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\, .\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054505 :question/id #uuid "cd630daa-4813-5ca8-b3d4-5fe35c4af4fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-192" :question/problem "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c52a76654a4a545d356890d4deb04c74301e4091422a51e84551aeb3ec1152.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m',$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y)\n\t&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\n\t(2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tm\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2}{-6}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, .\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $m'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty&=3x-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\t0&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\tx&=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 57 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675}] :topic/title "Implicit Differentiation"}]}
